  "cause": "On **22–23 September 2026**, the **Climate Impact Lab** — co-founded by **Prof. Michael Greenstone** of the University of Chicago — published a report titled **\"451,000 at Risk: Where Action Can Save Lives During the Super El Niño\"**, authored by **Michael Greenstone, Tamma Carleton, Genevieve Maricle and Emily Grover-Kopec**, released through the University of Chicago's **Institute for Climate and Sustainable Growth (EPIC)** and covered by **The Guardian** on 23 September. The report's findings are specific and dated: **global land temperatures are projected to be 1.2 °C (2.1 °F) above normal in the coming months, driven in large part by the \"Super El Niño\" that began in June and is expected to last through Northern Hemisphere spring** — and, on current forecasts, **through spring 2027**. That anomaly represents **44 % more extremely hot days than a normal year**, and the authors state the mechanism plainly: *\"El Niño is effectively pushing us into the future — exposing us to temperatures that won't be the norm for another two decades — before we have time to adapt and protect ourselves.\"* On that basis the report projects that **451,000 lives will be lost to heat in the first half of this El Niño** relative to a 1996–2025 baseline, including **239,000 ± 11,000 deaths in the next six months alone** — and it states its own scale in a single comparison: **that toll is about 65 times larger than the 6,800 people believed to be dead or missing from the recent Nepal flash flood.** The deaths fall overwhelmingly in the **Global South**: **66,800 additional heat deaths across the Sahel** with nearly half in **Nigeria (31,400)**, then **Sudan (17,500), Niger (10,000), Chad (8,000)**; **19,400 across the Philippines, Vietnam, Thailand and Cambodia**; **19,300 in Indonesia**; **India (15,800)**; and **Brazil (13,300)**. Greenstone's own framing of the number: *\"This is such a large number that it can be numbing, but it is made up of parents and children, grandparents and neighbours, people going to work, caring for families, and living their lives.\"* Indonesia is the place where the projection has already become visible. As of **Wednesday 24 September**, Indonesia's health ministry reported **more than 175,000 people suffering wildfire-related respiratory illness since August**, up from **50,891 on 1 September to 113,336 by 9 September** — the figure more than doubling in eight days (BBC, Independent). **Fires have been burning across Sumatra and Indonesian Borneo since mid-July, through one of the world's largest tropical peatland ecosystems**, and the resulting haze has spread to **Malaysia, Singapore and the Philippines**, in some areas driving pollution **more than 15 times the WHO recommended level** (BBC). **Nusantara Atlas had detected around 734,000 hotspots across the country as of Wednesday — more than 30 % higher than 2019, one of Indonesia's worst wildfire seasons — and at least 895,657 hectares have burned since January**, nearly six times the size of Greater London (Guardian). Authorities have deployed **more than 54,000 personnel and dozens of aircraft for water-bombing and cloud-seeding**, some operations conducted with **Japan's Self-Defense Forces**; **thousands of schools have closed and 167 psychiatric patients were evacuated from a hospital in South Kalimantan**; an **orangutan rescued in West Kalimantan died after treatment for hypoxia** (BBC, Independent). Forest-fire forensics expert **Prof. Bambang Hero Saharjo** estimated this year's El Niño could burn **as many as 11 million hectares — on a par with 1997–98**, Indonesia's worst season on record. Forestry minister **Raja Juli Antoni** said the fight was entering a critical phase: *\"Our problems are not over. September is the peak of El Niño. September is the main fight for us\"* (BBC). **The cause is a warming anomaly that is forecast months in advance and whose death toll is therefore known in advance — being met with firefighting, cloud-seeding and haze advisories rather than with the one thing that could still reduce the number.**",
