  "verse": "And do not reject people of your kind (next ones/fellow human beings) as the unfair ones (irresponsible ones) and the unrighteous ones (conscienceless ones) do, because they consider the others to be of less (lower) worth than themselves, indeed truly no-one amongst people of your kind (human beings) is more or less than another; truthly neither standing (rank) nor name nor goods and riches are of any importance (significance) as worth for people of your kind (human beings), just as the colour of skin, a belief, knowledge of the truth, descent from people (race) or shape (formation) of the body in the exterior or interior (physique and configuration of organs) are not of any significance either.",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 11, Verse 62",
  "hinge": "The cause is not the language and it is not the veil. It is the choice to write the foreignness of a child into a school's arithmetic — to make the child's measure of belonging a number that must be kept low — and to attach the duty of integration to the parent rather than to the school that holds the child for the hours in which language is learned. §62 does not ask the state to be indifferent to difference; it states that **no one is more or less than another, and that belief, descent and language are not of any significance as worth.** A cap is not a neutral application of that principle; it is its inversion, softened by the fact that the stated purpose is kind. Two decisions would change the effect entirely and neither requires abandoning the concern: guarantee intensive Italian instruction, funded per arriving pupil and delivered in school hours, instead of rationing access to it; and regulate garments by a rule that names no faith. Both are available this month. The political value of the decree lies in the first measure being the harder one to promise — and the law of cause and effect pays no attention to which of the two is more useful on a poster."
